[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator introduces the problem of prompting for camera angles and the solution offered by Figma Weave's new 3D capabilities.
AUDIO: 'Until now, creating with AI often meant prompting for the camera angle, composition or visual direction, and hoping you\'d get the result you had in mind. But as creatives, we want control. We want to move things around, tweak the composition and explore different angles, and direct every little detail of our shot. We\'ve just added some 3D capabilities that lets you do exactly that. With'

[00:16] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator discusses the desire for creative control in AI-generated content.
AUDIO: 'the New Worlds feature in Figma Weave, you can do exactly that. You can create a 3D world. Step inside it. Move your camera freely and choose exactly how you want to frame your shot. Let me'

[00:28] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A Figma Weave workflow is shown, featuring nodes for 3D worlds, ChatGPT Images 2.0 Edit, and a Compositor.
AUDIO: 'show you how it\'s done. We\'ll create a 3D world and learn how to navigate it. We\'ll design a character and turn it into a 3D model. We\'ll composite that character into our world and render our first frame. And finally, we\'ll turn those frames into a video and edit a complete'

[00:41] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator reiterates the benefits of the new 3D capabilities in Figma Weave.
AUDIO: 'sequence. For this tutorial, I\'m going to use a cinematic example, but this workflow can be applied to almost anything you want to direct product, videos, games, animation, fashion, and much more. Let\'s get started creating our world. First, we need a concept. As I mentioned, I\'m going to do'

[00:57] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A "World Creation" node in Figma Weave is shown with a detailed prompt and an input image for generating a 3D world.
AUDIO: 'something cinematic and realistic, but the world can be anything you imagine. And let me know in the comment what kind of worlds you\'d like to create. You can start with your sketch, an image, or'

[01:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simply a prompt. For my example, I decided to start with this image of an incredibly detailed house. I wanted something with a lot of depth, a place I\'d actually want to step inside and explore. So what I need to do now, once I\'ve got my image, is to connect the image to the World Labs model and'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just run it. The model now generates a full 3D Gaussian splat from my input. And just like that, I can step inside and start exploring. One thing that is important to understand about these worlds is because of the way that they are built. Some details might look a bit broken or blurry as you'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'move around, and that\'s fine. We\'re not using the 3D world as the final visual. Think of it as your digital set. We use it to understand the space, to explore different perspectives and position the camera and direct the shots. We\'ll control the final visual quality and style when we render'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'navigating the world. To navigate the world, we have two main cameras. One is the orbit camera. It lets us rotate around the model and explore it from different angles. Or we can switch to the fly camera and move freely through the environment using the keyboard. W moves forward, S moves backward, D'

[02:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the right and A to the left. I can also pan around using the middle mouse button and option or control depends on your computer. This is where you can really start. Think like a director. Move around through the environment and explore different compositions and find the exact frames'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want. Once we\'ve found the shot, we can move to the next stage. Creating a character. For this piece, I wanted to create a character that feels slightly out of place in the world, while also referencing my background in VFX. I started by generating an image of the character. My workflow'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uses an LLM to create a detailed description of the character, then passes that description into another LLM that turns it into a prompt for a full model sheet and turnaround. That turnaround is going to become useful just in a moment. Then I crop out the front view and use that image to'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generate a 3D model out of the character. Now we have both pieces. We have our set and our character so we can start bringing them together, rendering our first frame. First, I\'m gonna go back into my 3D world and position the camera and find the exact shot I want. Then I use the frame as an'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'input for an image to image model. In this case, GPT to create the final render to keep the visual style consistent. I\'ve also used an LLM to generate a style guide based on the original image. I use that style guide as my prompt for every time I\'m going to run a new frame. That means I can'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'keep changing my camera angle while using the same visual direction across all of my shots. And because we\'re using Weave, whenever I change my input, everything downstream will update automatically the next time I run the workflow, so I don\'t need to manually update everything. Once I'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have my high resolution render, I can send it into the compositor, and then I\'m going to bring in my 3D character, the one that we created with Meshy. Now I can place the character exactly where I want it in the frame, and I can move it, scale it and rotate it and adjust the camera until the'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'composition feels right. And when I change the camera in the 3D character view, it automatically updates inside the compositor. So once I\'m happy with the composition. I can run it with an image to image model again and get a final composited frame. Next we\'re going to turn our frames into'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video. Now we are ready for the final stage. I have all my frames ready, so all that\'s left is to turn them into moving shots. I connected each frame to Seedance and now I added a prompt describing the'

[04:34] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The Compositor timeline is shown, with multiple "Shot" layers, demonstrating the editing of a video sequence.
AUDIO: 'movement I want for that shot. Once I have all of my results, I bring everything back into the compositor and I start editing the sequence. Here I can control the pacing and timing of each shot until the whole sequence feel just right. And look where we started. We went from a single image to a'

[04:51] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator summarizes the workflow and its benefits.
AUDIO: 'complete 3D world. We explore that world and directed the camera\'s inside of it, created and added a character, render the frames and turn them into finished sequences. From here, I can color'

[05:03] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator concludes the tutorial, mentioning color correction and the Gen Effect node.
AUDIO: 'correct the sequence and add my final touches. In this case, I\'m also using the Gen Effect node to add subtle cinematic vignette across the entire sequence. If you want to learn more about the'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
